  "preamble": "Debuccalization of the aspirated dental stop [tʰ] removes the oral closure, and between vowels the remnant is the voiced glottal fricative [ɦ]. In the Prakrits single intervocalic th, like the other aspirates, became h, which Wikipedia transcribes /ɦ/ (Sanskrit kathanam /kɐt̪ʰɐn̪ɐm/ > Prakrit kahaṇaṃ /kɐɦɐnɐ̃/ 'saying'). Sauraseni often has voiced dh instead (ṇādha beside Maharashtri ṇāha).",
  "phoneticEffects": "Debuccalization, Lenition, Voicing",
  "languageExamples": [
    {
      "language": "Prakrit",
      "languageFamily": "Indo-Aryan",
      "examples": [
